Grand Theft Auto VI arrives on 19 November, and launch week brings a pile of dates, prices and claims, some of them wrong. Some earlier guides gave a pre-load date that Rockstar has since moved, and patch details have not been published. This guide sticks to what Rockstar, Microsoft and Take-Two have actually said, and flags what is still unknown.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer. Stackbrook has not played the game and has no affiliate relationship with Rockstar, Take-Two, Sony or Microsoft at the time of writing. This article contains no affiliate links. The AI check covered the official pages cited below and was not a full human review. The header image is AI-generated and is not official artwork.*

## The dates that matter

Rockstar's support pages put the launch at midnight local time on 19 November 2026, in all regions, on both PlayStation 5 and Xbox Series X|S. That means players in different time zones get the game at different real-world moments, rather than one global unlock.

The chart below collects the dates Rockstar has published.

![Timeline of confirmed GTA 6 dates: pre-orders opened 25 June, pre-load from 10 November, code-in-box copies from 12 November, launch at midnight local time on 19 November, bonus offers end before 20 November](/images/site1/gta-6-launch-week-what-is-confirmed-chart.svg) *Chart created by Stackbrook from Rockstar Games pages cited in this article.*

One date has changed from what many guides say. Rockstar's June announcement gave 12 November for pre-loading. Its 3 November newswire post later moved digital pre-loading to Tuesday 10 November, at midnight local time on PlayStation and 12 a.m. GMT on Xbox. If a site still tells you 12 November, it is out of date. Code-in-box copies are expected at retailers from 12 November, and Rockstar says codes can be used as soon as you have them.

The date itself has moved before. The game was first slated for fall 2025. Rockstar moved it to 26 May 2026 in a May 2025 announcement, then to 19 November 2026 in November 2025, saying it needed more time to finish the game. Take-Two's 9 November results release, the last before launch, kept the date.

## Platforms, editions and prices

The game launches on PlayStation 5 and Xbox Series X|S. Rockstar's support page lists only those two platforms and no PC version has been announced. Rockstar's pre-order announcement calls it a single-player experience, and the PlayStation listing describes no multiplayer modes. No online mode has been announced, so treat any online launch date as unconfirmed.

On pricing, Microsoft's Xbox Store lists the Standard Edition at $79.99 and the Ultimate Edition at $99.99 in the US. It also lists the Ultimate Edition Upgrade as a separate $20.00 add-on on its Xbox listing (PlayStation's page showed it as unavailable to pre-order), which fits Rockstar's statement that Standard Edition owners can buy the upgrade at any time. Rockstar says the Ultimate Edition adds extra in-game vehicles, weapons, clothing and items that unlock as the story progresses. Prices differ by country and store, so check yours.

Physical copies are not discs. Rockstar says the box contains only a download code, and that no physical Ultimate Edition exists, although physical owners can buy the upgrade from the PlayStation or Microsoft stores after redeeming their code.

## Pre-order bonuses and the GTA+ offer

Rockstar says digital pre-orders and purchases made before 20 November include the Vintage Vice City Pack. Its support page lists a '55 Vapid Stanier sedan with a garage, outfits and hairstyles, and an exclusive weapon pattern. Physical code copies carry the same pack.

Digital buyers also get one month of GTA+, subject to store, region and account limits. PlayStation's offer terms list several countries as excluded, including Qatar, and the offer is one per account. The terms differ by store. The PlayStation Store listing says the subscription auto-renews until cancelled and must be claimed by 31 March 2027, so set a reminder if you do not want to pay for a second month. The Xbox listing says the month must be redeemed within 180 days of ordering, and that the order must be placed before 19 November 23:59:59.

## Storage and pre-load

Rockstar says the pre-load needs up to 212 GB of available space depending on your device, so check your free space before anything else. Rockstar has not published a separate download size. Pre-loading puts the files on your console ahead of launch, and the game itself launches at the time above. Rockstar has not said whether a day-one patch exists or how large it would be, so figures you see for it are guesses.

Microsoft says it will also stream the game through Xbox Cloud Gaming from 1 a.m. ET on 19 November, for Xbox One and Series X|S, and has invited buyers to pre-load early. Microsoft calls the game the most pre-ordered title on its store, which is its own claim and is not independently verified here.

## What the business side says

Take-Two's 9 November results materials say pre-orders are tracking far above its initial expectations (the release itself says they continue to reach unprecedented levels), and keep its fiscal 2027 net bookings outlook at $8.0 billion to $8.2 billion. Its guidance for the October to December quarter is $3.35 billion to $3.5 billion in net bookings. Those are forecasts, not results, and Take-Two did not publish pre-order numbers.

## What is still not confirmed

- Whether a day-one patch exists, and how big it is.
- Any PC release date.
- Any online or multiplayer mode.
- Performance details such as frame rate and resolution, which we did not find in Rockstar's pages.

## A short checklist before 19 November

1. Check you have up to 212 GB of free space, and start or finish your pre-load now.
2. Make sure you bought from a store you recognise. Rockstar's own site links to the official stores.
3. If you claimed the GTA+ month, note the date it starts charging.
4. Treat any "leaked" patch size, key or early download link as untrustworthy until Rockstar publishes it.
5. Check Rockstar's support site on launch day for known issues.
